      <h1>Neural Networks</h1>
      <div class="statml-overview" aria-label="Lesson overview">
        <div class="statml-overview-item"><span class="statml-overview-number">1</span><span><strong>Prepare the evidence.</strong><br>Turn messy examples into usable features and honest data splits.</span></div>
        <div class="statml-overview-item"><span class="statml-overview-number">2</span><span><strong>Learn through error.</strong><br>See signals move forward and corrections move backward.</span></div>
        <div class="statml-overview-item"><span class="statml-overview-number">3</span><span><strong>Prove it works.</strong><br>Validate, test, deploy, monitor, and check fairness.</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="scroll-cue">↓ Scroll to begin</div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="lesson" class="statml-layout">
    <article class="statml-narrative">
      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="1" id="stage-1">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 01</div>
        <h2>The whole idea: learn patterns from examples</h2>
        <p>A <strong>neural network</strong> is a model made of small calculation units called neurons. Each neuron receives numbers, gives some of them more importance than others, combines them, and passes a new number forward.</p>
        <p>Imagine teaching a child to recognize cats. You do not write every possible cat rule. You show many examples labeled <span class="tag green">cat</span> or <span class="tag red">not cat</span>. The child gradually notices patterns. A neural network does something similar with numbers.</p>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">→</span><p><strong>Plain-English translation:</strong> examples go in, a prediction comes out, mistakes are measured, and the internal settings are adjusted so the next prediction is less wrong.</p></div>
        <div class="glass-card"><h3>Normal programming versus machine learning</h3><p><strong>Normal programming:</strong> a human writes the rules, the computer applies them, and an answer comes out. <strong>Machine learning:</strong> a human supplies examples and correct outcomes, then the model learns useful rules from those examples. Neural networks are valuable when the rules are too numerous or complicated to write by hand.</p></div>
        <div class="statml-prereq"><span class="content-kicker">Before you continue</span><p>You only need basic comfort with multiplication, averages, and percentages. Every specialist word is defined before it is used.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="2" id="stage-2">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 02</div>
        <h2>Data becomes features and labels</h2>
        <p><strong>Data</strong> is the collection of examples the model learns from. In our toy problem, each row describes one student.</p>
        <table class="mini-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Study hours</th><th>Sleep hours</th><th>Attendance</th><th>Passed?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="mono">8</td><td class="mono">7</td><td class="mono">90%</td><td><span class="tag green">Yes</span></td></tr><tr><td class="mono">2</td><td class="mono">4</td><td class="mono">40%</td><td><span class="tag red">No</span></td></tr><tr><td class="mono">5</td><td class="mono">6</td><td class="mono">70%</td><td><span class="tag green">Yes</span></td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="glass-card"><h3>Features</h3><p>The clues the model receives: study hours, sleep hours, and attendance.</p></div>
          <div class="glass-card"><h3>Label</h3><p>The correct answer it must learn to predict: passed or failed.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight-box"><p><strong>Supervised learning</strong> means the training examples already contain the correct answers. Features are the clues; the label is the answer key.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="3" id="stage-3">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 03</div>
        <h2>Clean, encode, scale, then split</h2>
        <p>Real data is rarely ready to train on. Missing values, duplicates, impossible values, inconsistent labels, and suspicious outliers must be investigated before the model sees them.</p>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div><h3>1. Clean</h3><p>Fix or remove records that are missing, duplicated, impossible, or inconsistent.</p></div>
          <div><h3>2. Encode</h3><p>Turn words and categories into numbers because the network performs mathematics.</p></div>
          <div><h3>3. Scale</h3><p>Put numerical features on comparable ranges so a large unit does not look important merely because its number is bigger.</p></div>
          <div><h3>4. Split</h3><p>Keep separate training, validation, and test sets so memorization cannot impersonate learning.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block">Training set   ≈ 70%  → teaches the model
Validation set ≈ 15%  → tunes choices during development
Test set       ≈ 15%  → final, untouched exam</div>
        <div class="warning-note"><p><strong>Keep the final exam separate:</strong> the test examples must not be used to teach or tune the model. Otherwise the final score no longer shows how well the model handles genuinely unseen data.</p></div>
        <div class="statml-rigor-panel"><p class="statml-rigor-kicker">Academic precision</p><h3>Why scaling is useful</h3><div class="statml-rigor-grid"><div><h4>What it prevents</h4><p>A feature measured in thousands can dominate numerical updates even when it is not more meaningful.</p></div><div><h4>What it does not mean</h4><p>Scaling does not automatically decide which feature matters. The network still learns weights from the data.</p></div></div></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="4" id="stage-4">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 04</div>
        <h2>A network is layers of tiny calculators</h2>
        <p>The <strong>input layer</strong> receives study, sleep, and attendance. Each hidden neuron receives its own weighted mixture of those inputs. Across training, different mixtures become sensitive to different combinations—perhaps high study with low sleep, or strong attendance with moderate study. These are <strong>emergent feature detectors</strong>: useful sensitivities discovered from data, not roles assigned to neurons by a person.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">Student pass/fail architecture: 3 → 8 → 4 → 1
3 inputs → hidden patterns → 1 pass-score neuron</div>
        <div class="choice-learn-panel" aria-label="What humans choose versus what the model learns">
          <div class="choice-learn-side">
            <span class="content-kicker">What you choose</span>
            <h3>Design decisions before training</h3>
            <ul><li>How many hidden layers</li><li>How many neurons per layer</li><li>Hidden and output activations</li><li>Optimizer and learning rate</li></ul>
          </div>
          <div class="choice-learn-side">
            <span class="content-kicker">What the model learns</span>
            <h3>Parameters discovered from examples</h3>
            <ul><li>Every connection weight</li><li>Every neuron bias</li><li>Useful internal sensitivities</li><li>Representations that reduce loss</li></ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">?</span><p><strong>Why not something simpler?</strong> A linear model may be enough when one straight boundary captures the pattern. Hidden layers are justified only when interactions and reusable intermediate patterns improve performance on new data.</p></div>
        <div class="insight-box"><p>The visual begins with the skeleton you designed, then animates the connection strengths that training learns. A larger network is not automatically better: more depth and width need more data, computation, and protection against overfitting.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="5" id="stage-5">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 05</div>
        <h2>One feature has a different weight into every hidden neuron</h2>
        <p>There is not one universal “sleep weight.” Sleep connects to every neuron in the next layer, and each connection learns a separate value. One neuron may respond strongly to sleep, another weakly, and another negatively because it combines sleep with different evidence.</p>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Connection explorer</span>
          <p>Choose an input to reveal its four independently learned connections.</p>
          <div class="button-row" role="group" aria-label="Choose a feature to inspect">
            <button class="btn" data-feature="study" aria-pressed="false">Study</button>
            <button class="btn active" data-feature="sleep" aria-pressed="true">Sleep</button>
            <button class="btn" data-feature="attendance" aria-pressed="false">Attendance</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="formula-block">hidden neuron j score = study × wstudy,j + sleep × wsleep,j + attendance × wattendance,j + biasj</div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">w</span><p><strong>What:</strong> weights control sensitivity and biases shift activation. <strong>How:</strong> training changes all of them to reduce loss. <strong>Why:</strong> separate weights let neurons specialize in different combinations. <strong>Why not assign roles?</strong> Human labels would constrain the model before the data reveals which combinations are useful.</p></div>
        <div class="insight-box"><p><strong>Interpretability note:</strong> you can inspect every weight, but raw numbers rarely translate into one clean human rule. A neuron usually participates in a distributed pattern with many other neurons.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="6" id="stage-6">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 06</div>
        <h2>Non-linearity lets stacked layers learn bends</h2>
        <p>First ask why an activation is needed. If every layer performs only a linear weighted sum, stacking ten layers still collapses into one linear transformation. The network gains more arithmetic, but not a more flexible decision boundary.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">inputs → weighted sum + bias → raw score → activation function → output signal</div>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Boundary experiment</span>
          <div class="button-row" role="group" aria-label="Toggle the hidden-layer ReLU">
            <button class="btn" data-relu="off" aria-pressed="false">Linear only</button>
            <button class="btn active" data-relu="on" aria-pressed="true">ReLU on</button>
          </div>
          <p>Use the buttons to focus either path; both remain visible for a direct comparison. Hidden layers use ReLU by default here. The output activation changes with the task:</p>
          <div class="button-row" role="group" aria-label="Choose output task">
            <button class="btn active" data-task="binary" aria-pressed="true">Binary sigmoid</button>
            <button class="btn" data-task="multiclass" aria-pressed="false">Multiclass softmax</button>
            <button class="btn" data-task="regression" aria-pressed="false">Regression linear</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">↳</span><p><strong>ReLU is a learned-pattern gate.</strong> A positive raw score passes as evidence that one learned pattern is present; zero means that neuron does not pass evidence for that pattern. It is misleading to say the network simply “throws away negative information”: other neurons—with different weights and biases—can represent opposing or complementary evidence.</p></div>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div><h3>Hidden layers: ReLU</h3><p><code>ReLU(2.5) = 2.5</code> passes positive evidence; <code>ReLU(-3) = 0</code> gates that detector off.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Binary output: sigmoid</h3><p>One raw output becomes a score from 0 to 1, such as a pass score of <code>0.82</code>.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Multiclass output: softmax</h3><p>Several class scores are converted together into probabilities that total 100%.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Regression output: linear</h3><p>The output remains a direct number because a probability constraint would be inappropriate.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="warning-note"><p><strong>Design decision:</strong> humans choose activations before training. Training learns the weights and biases that determine which patterns produce positive or non-positive raw scores.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="7" id="stage-7">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 07</div>
        <h2>Forward propagation makes one pass-score prediction</h2>
        <p><strong>Forward propagation</strong> means information moves from the three inputs, through every hidden layer, to the single output neuron used by this binary task.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">study + sleep + attendance
→ hidden-layer calculations and ReLU activations
→ one raw output score
→ sigmoid
→ pass score between 0 and 1</div>
        <p>For one student, the output neuron might produce <strong>0.82</strong>. We read that as an 82% model score for “pass.” The corresponding fail score is <code>1 − 0.82 = 0.18</code>.</p>
        <div class="insight-box"><p>The output neuron does not yet make the final Pass/Fail decision. Stage 14 will apply a separate decision threshold, such as 0.50, to turn this continuous score into a class label.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="8" id="stage-8">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 08</div>
        <h2>Loss measures how wrong the prediction was</h2>
        <p>A <strong>loss function</strong> converts prediction error into a number. Smaller loss is better. Larger loss tells the training process that the current weights need more correction.</p>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="glass-card"><h3>Regression</h3><p>When predicting a number, <strong>mean squared error</strong> is common. It squares mistakes, so large errors receive extra punishment.</p></div>
          <div class="glass-card"><h3>Classification</h3><p>When predicting a class, <strong>cross-entropy loss</strong> is common. It strongly penalizes a model that is confidently wrong.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">!</span><p><strong>Loss is the teacher's marking pen.</strong> It does not merely say “wrong.” It gives the optimizer a numerical signal showing how much improvement is needed.</p></div>
        <div class="statml-rigor-panel"><p class="statml-rigor-kicker">Do not confuse these</p><h3>Loss versus metric</h3><div class="statml-rigor-grid"><div><h4>Loss</h4><p>The quantity optimized during training. It guides weight updates.</p></div><div><h4>Metric</h4><p>A human-readable measure such as accuracy, precision, recall, MAE, or RMSE.</p></div></div></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="9" id="stage-9">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 09</div>
        <h2>Backpropagation computes gradients—only gradients</h2>
        <p>A <strong>gradient</strong> is a collection of slopes: for each weight, it answers “if this weight increased a tiny amount, how would the loss change?” The sign gives a direction and the magnitude describes local sensitivity.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">forward pass → prediction
prediction + truth → loss
backpropagation → gradient for every weight and bias
weights are still unchanged</div>
        <p>Backpropagation applies the chain rule from the loss backward through the network. It calculates how local changes connect to the final error. It does <strong>not</strong> choose a learning rate and it never updates a parameter.</p>
        <div class="warning-note"><p><strong>Plain distinction:</strong> backprop computes gradients only. The optimizer later uses those gradients to update weights.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="10" id="stage-10">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 10</div>
        <h2>The optimizer takes iterative, local steps</h2>
        <p><strong>Gradient descent</strong> applies the update. It combines the local gradient with a <strong>learning rate</strong>: <code>next weight = current weight − learning rate × gradient</code>. Because the gradient describes only the slope near the current point, optimization must repeat rather than jump directly to the answer.</p>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Watch it walk downhill</span>
          <div class="button-row" role="group" aria-label="Choose learning-rate behavior">
            <button class="btn" data-lr-mode="tiny" aria-pressed="false">Tiny steps</button>
            <button class="btn active" data-lr-mode="medium" aria-pressed="true">Medium steps</button>
            <button class="btn" data-lr-mode="large" aria-pressed="false">Too large</button>
            <button class="btn" id="optimPause" aria-pressed="false">Pause</button>
          </div>
          <p id="lrHelp"><strong>Small steps</strong> need many updates. <strong>Medium steps</strong> descend efficiently. <strong>Large steps</strong> can overshoot and bounce away. Drag the current point on the curve to inspect a different local slope.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">∇</span><p><strong>What:</strong> a gradient is local slope. <strong>Why:</strong> it tells the optimizer which nearby direction lowers loss. <strong>How:</strong> multiply it by the learning rate and update. <strong>Why not one giant step?</strong> the landscape bends, and the local slope does not reveal the whole terrain.</p></div>
        <details class="deep-dive"><summary>Why local does not guarantee global</summary><div class="deep-content"><p>A low point reached from the current start may be a <strong>local minimum</strong>, while a lower <strong>global minimum</strong> exists elsewhere. Initialization, optimizer behavior, noise from batches, and the shape of the loss surface all affect the path.</p></div></details>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="11" id="stage-11">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 11</div>
        <h2>One update has a strict causal order</h2>
        <p>The model usually learns from a small <strong>batch</strong>. Each update follows the same sequence; confusing these steps hides what actually causes learning.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">1. take data
2. forward pass makes predictions
3. loss measures error
4. backpropagation computes gradients
5. optimizer applies parameter updates
6. updated weights change the next prediction
7. repeat</div>
        <div class="metric-row"><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value">Batch</div><div class="metric-label">examples per update</div></div><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value">Iteration</div><div class="metric-label">one update step</div></div><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value">Epoch</div><div class="metric-label">one full pass through training data</div></div></div>
        <div class="formula-block">1,000 examples ÷ batch size 100 = 10 iterations per epoch</div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">↻</span><p>The forward pass supplies a prediction; loss supplies an objective; backprop supplies gradients; the optimizer supplies the update. Repetition turns many local corrections into learned behavior.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="12" id="stage-12">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 12</div>
        <h2>Choose the model before validation gets worse</h2>
        <p>Training loss can keep falling while validation loss eventually rises. That is the causal signature of overfitting: the model is improving on examples it trains on while becoming less useful on unseen data. Keep the checkpoint near the lowest validation loss, not the final training step.</p>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">⌁</span><p><strong>Local is not global:</strong> an optimizer follows nearby slopes and may settle in one valley even if a lower valley exists elsewhere. Validation asks a different question: whichever valley training reaches, does that model generalize?</p></div>
        <div class="plain-translation"><span class="plain-icon">!</span><p><strong>Why can dependence be bad?</strong> A neuron might learn a brittle shortcut such as “attendance decides everything.” If later neurons rely heavily on that one pathway, the network may fail when the shortcut does not hold for new students.</p></div>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Regularization control</span>
          <label for="dropoutRate">Dropout rate during training: <span class="control-readout" id="dropoutReadout">20%</span></label>
          <input id="dropoutRate" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="20" aria-describedby="dropoutHelp">
          <p id="dropoutHelp">Decrease the rate and more hidden neurons stay active on the right. Increase it and more paths switch off, which can reduce shortcut dependence but may underfit; at 100%, this simplified visual switches off every hidden neuron.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div><h3>During training</h3><p>Different hidden neurons are temporarily switched off on different updates.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Validation and real use</h3><p>Dropout is switched off, the full network is used, and the framework handles the required scaling.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Decrease dropout</h3><p>More paths stay active, but the model may overfit by leaning on shortcuts.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Increase dropout</h3><p>More paths switch off in the visual, spreading reliance until excessive dropout starts to underfit.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="warning-note"><p><strong>Keep this model:</strong> save the checkpoint at the validation minimum. Dropout is one possible regularizer, not an automatic requirement; its value depends on the architecture, data, and observed overfitting.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="13" id="stage-13">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 13</div>
        <h2>Humans choose the architecture and hyperparameters</h2>
        <p><strong>Parameters</strong> are learned by the model: every weight and bias. <strong>Hyperparameters</strong> are design decisions made before or around training.</p>
        <div class="statml-rigor-panel"><p class="statml-rigor-kicker">Design decisions</p><h3>Why each choice exists</h3><div class="statml-rigor-grid"><div><h4>Depth and width</h4><p>Control representational capacity. Too little can underfit; too much can waste data and computation or overfit.</p></div><div><h4>Activations</h4><p>Add non-linearity and match output constraints. Hidden layers remain ReLU here.</p></div><div><h4>Optimizer</h4><p>Defines how gradients are converted into parameter updates.</p></div><div><h4>Learning rate</h4><p>Sets update scale: small is slow; large can overshoot.</p></div></div></div>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Output-layer explorer</span>
          <div class="button-row" role="group" aria-label="Choose prediction task">
            <button class="btn active" data-task="binary" aria-pressed="true">Binary class</button>
            <button class="btn" data-task="multiclass" aria-pressed="false">Multi-class</button>
            <button class="btn" data-task="regression" aria-pressed="false">Regression</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-grid"><div><h3>Binary classification</h3><p>Hidden layers: ReLU. Output: one neuron with sigmoid.</p></div><div><h3>Multi-class classification</h3><p>Hidden layers: ReLU. Output: one score per class with softmax.</p></div><div><h3>Regression</h3><p>Hidden layers: ReLU. Output: usually one linear numerical value.</p></div><div><h3>Feature engineering</h3><p>Structured problems may improve when raw fields are turned into more useful clues, such as date of birth → age.</p></div></div>
        <div class="warning-note"><p>There is no universal “best” architecture. A design must match the task, data quantity, noise, complexity, and cost of mistakes.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="14" id="stage-14">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 14</div>
        <h2>The test set evaluates both scores and decisions</h2>
        <p>After training and tuning are finished, the model is evaluated once on the untouched <strong>test set</strong>. For our binary task, sigmoid first produces a pass score. A separate <strong>classification decision threshold</strong> then converts that score into Pass or Fail.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">model pass score = 0.72
decision threshold = 0.50
0.72 ≥ 0.50
prediction = Pass</div>
        <div class="warning-note"><p>This threshold is <strong>not the school’s exam passing mark</strong>. It is the model’s cutoff for turning a score between 0 and 1 into a predicted class.</p></div>
        <div class="control-card"><span class="content-kicker">Decision control</span><label for="threshold">Classification decision threshold: <span class="control-readout" id="thresholdReadout">0.50</span></label><input id="threshold" type="range" min="20" max="80" value="50" aria-describedby="thresholdHelp"><p id="thresholdHelp">Lowering the threshold predicts Pass more easily: recall usually rises, but false positives may increase. Raising it is stricter: false positives may fall, but more actual passes can be missed.</p></div>
        <div class="metric-row"><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value" id="thresholdPrecision">0.75</div><div class="metric-label">Precision</div></div><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value" id="thresholdRecall">0.75</div><div class="metric-label">Recall</div></div><div class="metric-card"><div class="metric-value" id="thresholdF1">0.75</div><div class="metric-label">F1-score</div></div></div>
        <table class="mini-table"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Actually passed</th><th>Actually failed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Predicted pass</th><td><strong>True positive:</strong> <span id="tpCount">3</span></td><td><strong>False positive:</strong> <span id="fpCount">1</span></td></tr><tr><th>Predicted fail</th><td><strong>False negative:</strong> <span id="fnCount">1</span></td><td><strong>True negative:</strong> <span id="tnCount">3</span></td></tr></tbody></table>
        <div class="insight-box"><p><strong>Accuracy alone can mislead.</strong> Precision asks whether predicted passes were usually correct. Recall asks how many actual passes the model successfully found.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="15" id="stage-15">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 15</div>
        <h2>Error analysis starts with individual students</h2>
        <p>Overall scores hide which people are affected. Move the threshold, inspect the highlighted false positives and false negatives, and connect each case to the metric that represents its cost.</p>
        <div class="control-card">
          <span class="content-kicker">Case-level threshold explorer</span>
          <label for="errorThreshold">Classification threshold: <span class="control-readout" id="errorThresholdReadout">0.50</span></label>
