        <div class="stage-label">Stage 16</div>
        <h2>Saving, deployment, and monitoring finish the lifecycle</h2>
        <p>Once performance is acceptable, the final model is saved. The saved package includes the architecture, learned weights and biases, and often the preprocessing rules needed to reproduce training-time inputs.</p>
        <div class="formula-block">new user input
→ same preprocessing used during training
→ saved model
→ prediction
→ application response</div>
        <p><strong>Deployment</strong> puts the model into a website, mobile app, server API, edge device, or business system. A model sitting in a notebook is an experiment, not a working service.</p>
        <div class="statml-deepening"><span class="content-kicker">After launch</span><p>Monitor accuracy, error types, input-data changes, speed, complaints, fairness, and security. When the world changes and performance falls, the model is experiencing <strong>drift</strong>.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="statml-stage" data-stage="17" id="stage-17">
        <div class="stage-label">Stage 17</div>
        <h2>Different network families organize information differently</h2>
        <div class="compare-grid"><div><h3>Feedforward network</h3><p>Information moves from input to hidden layers to output. This is the structure used throughout this lesson.</p></div><div><h3>Convolutional network (CNN)</h3><p>Common for images. Early layers notice edges; later layers combine them into shapes and object parts.</p></div><div><h3>Recurrent network (RNN)</h3><p>Designed for sequences and previous context, such as text, audio, and time series.</p></div><div><h3>Transformer</h3><p>Uses attention to connect relevant parts of data, allowing many relationships to be considered at once.</p></div></div>
        <p>This layered transformation is called <strong>representation learning</strong>. Raw inputs become increasingly useful internal patterns: pixels → edges → shapes → objects, or word pieces → phrases → context.</p>
        <div class="warning-note"><p><strong>Interpretability:</strong> weights are visible, but a representation is usually distributed across many parameters. Inspecting one large weight rarely yields a faithful human rule; use case-level explanations, sensitivity analyses, and validation evidence together.</p></div>
        <div class="insight-box"><p>Neural networks are powerful because they can learn complex representations. They are used for images, speech, text, translation, recommendations, medical scans, fraud detection, forecasting, game playing, and large language models. They are not magic: they still depend on good data, sensible design, testing, monitoring, and human judgment.</p></div>
      </section>

